export const coreArticles = {
  "os-processes-threads": "# Processes and Threads\n\n## What is a Process?\n\nA **process** is a program that is currently being executed by the computer. When you double-click a program icon, the operating system creates a process for it. The program on disk is just a passive file \u2014 the process is the active, running version.\n\nEvery process gets its own:\n- **Memory space** (code, data, heap, stack)\n- **Program Counter** (which instruction to execute next)\n- **CPU Registers** (current computation state)\n- **File descriptors** (open files, network connections)\n- **Process ID (PID)** \u2014 a unique number the OS uses to track it\n\nThink of a process like a sealed container. Nothing outside can touch what's inside without going through the OS.\n\n---\n\n## What is a Thread?\n\nA **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process. A process can have one or many threads. Threads within the same process **share** the process's memory \u2014 they see the same code, heap, and global variables.\n\nHowever, each thread has its own:\n- **Stack** (local variables and function call chain)\n- **Stack Pointer**\n- **Program Counter** (where this thread is executing)\n- **Thread ID**\n\n**Analogy:** A restaurant is a process. Each waiter is a thread. They all work in the same kitchen (shared memory) but each has their own order pad (stack).\n\n---\n\n## Process vs Thread \u2014 Full Comparison\n\n| Feature | Process | Thread |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory | Own private address space | Shared with other threads in same process |\n| Creation cost | High (allocates memory, copies resources) | Low (~10\u00d7 cheaper than process) |\n| Communication | Requires IPC (pipes, sockets, shared memory) | Direct \u2014 just read/write shared memory |\n| Crash impact | Crash kills only that process | Thread crash can kill the whole process |\n| Context switch | Expensive (must save/restore full memory map) | Cheaper (only save/restore registers and stack) |\n| Data sharing | Processes are isolated by default | Threads share data naturally (needs synchronization) |\n\n---\n\n## Process Life Cycle \u2014 The 5 States\n\nEvery process moves through these states during its lifetime:\n\n```\nNew \u2500\u2500\u25ba Ready \u2500\u2500\u25ba Running \u2500\u2500\u25ba Terminated\n              \u25b2       \u2502\n              \u2502       \u25bc\n              \u2514\u2500\u2500 Waiting (Blocked)\n```\n\n| State | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **New** | Process is being created (not yet in ready queue) |\n| **Ready** | In memory, waiting for CPU time |\n| **Running** | Currently executing on CPU |\n| **Waiting/Blocked** | Waiting for I/O or an event (disk, network, user input) |\n| **Terminated** | Finished execution; resources being reclaimed |\n\n**Key transitions:**\n- Running \u2192 Waiting: Process requests I/O (e.g., reads a file)\n- Waiting \u2192 Ready: I/O completes; OS puts process back in ready queue\n- Running \u2192 Ready: Time quantum expired (preemption) or higher-priority process arrives\n\n---\n\n## Process Control Block (PCB)\n\nThe PCB is the data structure the OS uses to store all information about a process. Think of it as the process's ID card.\n\nA PCB contains:\n- Process ID (PID)\n- Process State (new/ready/running/waiting/terminated)\n- Program Counter\n- CPU registers (saved when process is paused)\n- Memory management info (page tables, base/limit registers)\n- Open file descriptors\n- Accounting info (CPU time used, priority)\n\n**Why does PCB matter?** When the OS pauses one process and starts another (context switch), it saves the PCB of the paused process and loads the PCB of the next process.\n\n---\n\n## Context Switching\n\n**Context switch** = the OS pauses one process/thread and resumes another.\n\nSteps:\n1. Save the running process's state into its PCB\n2. Pick the next process from the ready queue (scheduler decides)\n3. Load that process's PCB (registers, PC, memory info)\n4. Resume execution\n\nContext switches take time \u2014 typically 1\u201310 microseconds. Too many context switches slow the system down (this is called **context switch overhead**).\n\n---\n\n## Types of Threads\n\n### User-Level Threads\nManaged by a user-space thread library (not the OS kernel). Fast to create and switch. Disadvantage: if one thread blocks on I/O, the whole process blocks.\n\n### Kernel-Level Threads\nManaged by the OS kernel. OS can schedule individual threads on different CPUs. Disadvantage: slower to create and switch than user threads.\n\n### Hybrid Threads\nCombines both \u2014 multiple user threads mapped to some kernel threads. Used by modern OSes.\n\n---\n\n## Special Process Types\n\n**Zombie Process:** A process that has finished execution but whose PCB entry still exists because its parent hasn't called ",
  "os-cpu-scheduling": "# CPU Scheduling\n\n## What is CPU Scheduling?\n\nAt any moment, multiple processes may be ready to run. The CPU can only run one at a time. The **CPU scheduler** (also called the short-term scheduler) decides which process in the Ready Queue gets the CPU next.\n\nGood scheduling = high CPU utilization + fast response time + fair treatment of all processes.\n\n---\n\n## Key Performance Metrics\n\n| Metric | Definition | Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Arrival Time (AT)** | When process enters ready queue | Given |\n| **Burst Time (BT)** | How long it needs CPU | Given |\n| **Completion Time (CT)** | When process finishes | Observed |\n| **Turnaround Time (TAT)** | Total time from arrival to completion | CT \u2212 AT |\n| **Waiting Time (WT)** | Time spent in ready queue (not running) | TAT \u2212 BT |\n| **Response Time** | Time from arrival to first CPU execution | First run \u2212 AT |\n\n**Goal:** Minimize average WT and TAT. Maximize CPU utilization.\n\n---\n\n## Algorithm 1 \u2014 FCFS (First Come First Served)\n\n**Rule:** Whoever arrives first gets CPU first. Non-preemptive.\n\n**Example:** 3 processes arrive at t=0: P1 (BT=10), P2 (BT=5), P3 (BT=8)\n- Gantt chart: |P1(0-10)|P2(10-15)|P3(15-23)|\n- WT: P1=0, P2=10, P3=15 \u2192 Avg WT = 8.33\n\n**Problem \u2014 Convoy Effect:** Short processes get stuck behind a very long one. If P1 had BT=100, P2 and P3 wait 100 seconds even though they only need 5 and 8 seconds.\n\n---\n\n## Algorithm 2 \u2014 SJF (Shortest Job First)\n\n**Rule:** Process with smallest burst time runs next. Non-preemptive.\n\n**Optimal:** Gives minimum average waiting time among non-preemptive algorithms.\n\n**Example:** P1 (BT=10, AT=0), P2 (BT=5, AT=2), P3 (BT=3, AT=4)\nAt t=0, only P1 available \u2192 runs. At t=10: P2 and P3 available. Shortest=P3.\n- Gantt: |P1(0-10)|P3(10-13)|P2(13-18)|\n- WT: P1=0, P2=11, P3=6 \u2192 Avg WT = 5.67\n\n**Problem \u2014 Starvation:** Long processes may never run if shorter ones keep arriving.\n\n---\n\n## Algorithm 3 \u2014 SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First)\n\n**Rule:** Preemptive version of SJF. New process preempts current if it has shorter remaining time.\n\n**Optimal:** Gives minimum average waiting time among ALL scheduling algorithms.\n\n---\n\n## Algorithm 4 \u2014 Round Robin (RR)\n\n**Rule:** Each process gets a fixed **time quantum (Q)** on the CPU. After Q, it goes to the back of the ready queue. Preemptive and fair.\n\n**Example:** Q=3, Processes: P1(BT=7), P2(BT=4), P3(BT=9)\n- Gantt: |P1(0-3)|P2(3-6)|P3(6-9)|P1(9-12)|P2(12-13)|P3(13-16)|P1(16-19)|P3(19-22)|\n\n**Choosing Q:** \n- Q too small \u2192 too many context switches (overhead dominates)\n- Q too large \u2192 degenerates to FCFS\n- Ideal Q is slightly larger than average burst time\n\n**Best for:** Interactive/time-sharing systems. Users get fast responses.\n\n---\n\n## Algorithm 5 \u2014 Priority Scheduling\n\n**Rule:** Each process has a priority number. Highest priority runs first. Can be preemptive or non-preemptive.\n\n**Problem \u2014 Starvation:** Low-priority processes may wait indefinitely.\n\n**Solution \u2014 Aging:** Gradually increase the priority of processes that have been waiting a long time. Eventually even a low-priority process gets high enough priority to run.\n\n---\n\n## Comparison Table\n\n| Algorithm | Preemptive? | Starvation? | Best Use Case |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| FCFS | No | No | Batch systems |\n| SJF | No | Yes | Batch; minimizes avg WT |\n| SRTF | Yes | Yes | Theoretical optimum |\n| Round Robin | Yes | No | Time-sharing, interactive |\n| Priority | Yes/No | Yes | Real-time systems |\n\n---\n\n## Gantt Chart \u2014 How to Draw\n\nFor exam questions, always draw the Gantt chart first, then calculate metrics from it.\n\n1. List all processes with AT, BT, Priority\n2. At each time step, apply the scheduling rule to pick the running process\n3. Draw the execution timeline\n4. Calculate CT, TAT, WT for each process\n5. Compute averages\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Metric | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Turnaround Time | CT \u2212 AT |\n| Waiting Time | TAT \u2212 BT |\n| Response Time | First execution start \u2212 AT |\n| CPU Utilization | Busy time / Total time \u00d7 100 |\n| Throughput | Processes completed per unit time |\n",
  "os-deadlock": "# Deadlock\n\n## What is Deadlock?\n\nDeadlock is a situation where a group of processes are **permanently blocked**, each waiting for a resource held by another process in the group. No process can proceed, and none will release its resources.\n\n**Classic analogy:** Two cars approach a one-lane bridge from opposite sides. Car A is on the bridge waiting for Car B to back up. Car B is on the bridge waiting for Car A to back up. Neither moves \u2014 deadlock.\n\n---\n\n## The Four Coffman Conditions\n\n**Deadlock can ONLY occur when ALL four conditions hold simultaneously.** Remove any one \u2192 deadlock cannot happen.\n\n| Condition | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Mutual Exclusion** | At least one resource must be non-shareable (only one process at a time) |\n| **2. Hold and Wait** | A process holds at least one resource while waiting to acquire additional resources |\n| **3. No Preemption** | Resources cannot be forcibly taken from a process; they must be voluntarily released |\n| **4. Circular Wait** | A circular chain: P1 waits for P2, P2 waits for P3, ..., Pn waits for P1 |\n\n---\n\n## Resource Allocation Graph (RAG)\n\nA RAG is a directed graph that visualizes resource allocation:\n- **Circle** = Process (P1, P2...)\n- **Square** = Resource (R1, R2...)\n- **Arrow from Process to Resource** = Process is waiting for resource (request edge)\n- **Arrow from Resource to Process** = Resource is assigned to process (assignment edge)\n\n**Detecting deadlock with RAG:**\n- If the RAG has a **cycle** AND each resource has **only one instance** \u2192 **deadlock exists**\n- If resources have multiple instances, a cycle doesn't guarantee deadlock \u2014 need Banker's algorithm\n\n---\n\n## Deadlock Handling Strategies\n\n### Strategy 1 \u2014 Prevention (Eliminate a Coffman Condition)\n\n| Condition to Break | How |\n|---|---|\n| Mutual Exclusion | Allow sharing (not always possible \u2014 printers can't be shared) |\n| Hold and Wait | Require processes to request ALL resources before starting |\n| No Preemption | Allow OS to forcibly take resources (used in CPU preemption) |\n| Circular Wait | Impose a total ordering on resource types; processes must request in order |\n\n**Hold and Wait prevention:** Process declares all needed resources upfront. If any unavailable, it waits without holding anything. Problem: low resource utilization; process may not know all resources in advance.\n\n**Circular Wait prevention (most practical):** Number all resources. A process can only request resource Rj if j > all resources it currently holds. This ensures no cycle can form.\n\n### Strategy 2 \u2014 Avoidance (Banker's Algorithm)\n\nThe OS maintains a \"safe state\" \u2014 a state from which it can guarantee all processes will eventually complete.\n\nBefore granting a resource request, the OS runs a simulation: if this request is granted, can we still complete all processes? If yes \u2192 **grant**. If no \u2192 **make the process wait**.\n\n**Safe State Definition:** There exists a safe sequence \u2014 an ordering of processes P1, P2, ..., Pn such that each Pi's remaining resource needs can be met by:\n- Currently available resources +\n- Resources held by all Pj where j < i\n\n**Banker's Algorithm Steps:**\n1. Maintain: Available[], Allocation[][], Max[][], Need[][]\n2. Where Need[i][j] = Max[i][j] \u2212 Allocation[i][j]\n3. For each request: temporarily grant it, check for safe sequence, grant only if safe\n\n### Strategy 3 \u2014 Detection and Recovery\n\nAllow deadlock to occur, detect it (periodically run detection algorithm on RAG or using Wait-For Graph), then recover.\n\n**Recovery options:**\n- **Kill all deadlocked processes** (drastic)\n- **Kill one at a time** until deadlock is broken\n- **Preempt resources** from some processes and give to others\n\n### Strategy 4 \u2014 Ostrich Algorithm\n\nIgnore deadlock. Used in most general-purpose OS (Linux, Windows) for the trade-off: deadlocks are rare, and detection overhead isn't worth it. When deadlock occurs, the user restarts the stuck program.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Topic | Key Point |\n|---|---|\n| 4 Conditions | Mutual Exclusion, Hold-Wait, No Preemption, Circular Wait |\n| RAG cycle + 1 instance | Deadlock certain |\n| Banker's Algorithm | Avoidance by maintaining safe state |\n| Prevention | Break one of the 4 conditions |\n| Safe sequence | Order where each process can get resources and complete |\n",
  "os-memory-management": "# Memory Management\n\n## Why Memory Management?\n\nMultiple processes run simultaneously, each needing memory. The OS must:\n1. Keep processes isolated from each other (no accidental overwrites)\n2. Give each process the illusion of large, contiguous memory\n3. Efficiently use the physical RAM available\n\n---\n\n## Logical vs Physical Address\n\n**Logical (Virtual) Address:** The address a process uses in its code. Each process starts at address 0 in its own address space.\n\n**Physical Address:** The actual location in RAM.\n\nThe **Memory Management Unit (MMU)** in hardware translates logical \u2192 physical at runtime. The OS sets up the translation tables.\n\n---\n\n## Paging\n\n**Idea:** Divide both physical memory and logical memory into fixed-size blocks.\n- Physical blocks = **Frames**\n- Logical blocks = **Pages**\n- Page size = Frame size (typically 4KB)\n\n**Page Table:** A per-process table that maps each page number \u2192 frame number.\n\n**Address Translation:**\n- Logical address = Page Number (p) + Offset (d)\n- Physical address = Frame Number (f, from page table) + Offset (d)\n\n**Advantages:**\n- No external fragmentation (frames can be anywhere in physical memory)\n- Easy to implement\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Internal fragmentation: last page may not be fully used\n- Page table can be large (for 32-bit with 4KB pages: 2\u00b2\u2070 entries!)\n\n---\n\n## Segmentation\n\n**Idea:** Divide memory into variable-size **segments** \u2014 each representing a logical unit (code segment, data segment, stack segment).\n\n**Segment Table:** Maps segment number \u2192 (base address, limit).\n\n**Address Translation:**\n- Logical = Segment Number (s) + Offset (d)\n- Check: d < limit[s] (bounds check)\n- Physical = base[s] + d\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Segments match programmer's view of memory\n- Different protection for different segments (code = read-only, data = read-write)\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- External fragmentation (variable-size segments create holes in memory)\n\n---\n\n## Paging vs Segmentation\n\n| Feature | Paging | Segmentation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Division | Fixed-size pages | Variable-size segments |\n| Programmer visible? | No (transparent) | Yes (code, stack, data) |\n| Internal fragmentation | Yes | No |\n| External fragmentation | No | Yes |\n| Protection | Page-level | Segment-level |\n\nModern systems use **Segmentation with Paging** (segments divided into pages).\n\n---\n\n## Virtual Memory and Demand Paging\n\n**Virtual Memory:** Give each process the illusion of having more memory than physically available, by using disk as an extension of RAM.\n\n**Demand Paging:** Pages are loaded into RAM only when accessed. If a page isn't in RAM:\n1. **Page Fault** occurs \u2014 CPU traps to OS\n2. OS finds the page on disk\n3. OS loads it into a free frame (may need to evict another page)\n4. Updates page table\n5. Resumes the process\n\n---\n\n## Page Replacement Algorithms\n\nWhen all frames are full and a page fault occurs, which page to evict?\n\n### FIFO (First In First Out)\nEvict the page that has been in memory the longest.\n- Simple but not always optimal\n- Suffers from **Belady's Anomaly**: more frames can cause MORE page faults!\n\n### LRU (Least Recently Used)\nEvict the page that hasn't been used for the longest time.\n- Near-optimal in practice\n- Does NOT suffer from Belady's Anomaly\n- Expensive to implement (needs tracking of access times)\n\n### Optimal (OPT/Belady's)\nEvict the page that won't be used for the longest time in the future.\n- Theoretical optimum \u2014 requires future knowledge\n- Used as a benchmark to measure other algorithms\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Algorithm | Page Faults | Belady's Anomaly | Practical? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| FIFO | High | Yes | Yes |\n| LRU | Low | No | Yes (approximated) |\n| OPT | Minimum | No | No (needs future) |\n\n---\n\n## TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer)\n\nPage table lookups require memory access \u2014 this would halve CPU speed. The **TLB** is a fast cache (hardware) for page table entries.\n\n**TLB Hit:** Physical address found in TLB \u2192 very fast (1 extra cycle)\n**TLB Miss:** Must go to page table in memory \u2192 slower (2 memory accesses)\n\n**Effective Access Time (EAT):**\n> EAT = hit_rate \u00d7 (TLB time + 1 memory access) + miss_rate \u00d7 (TLB time + 2 memory accesses)\n\n**TLB hit rate** is typically 95\u201399% in real workloads, making paging practical.\n\n---\n\n## Fragmentation\n\n| Type | Description | Caused By |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Internal** | Wasted space INSIDE an allocated block | Paging (last page not full) |\n| **External** | Wasted space BETWEEN allocated blocks | Segmentation, variable-size allocation |\n| **Compaction** | OS moves all processes to eliminate external fragmentation | Expensive; requires stopping processes |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Concept | Key Point |\n|---|---|\n| Paging | Fixed-size pages; no external fragmentation |\n| Segmentation | Variable-size; no internal fragmentation |\n| Page fault | Page not in RAM; fetch from disk |\n| FIFO | May have Belady's Anomaly |\n| LRU | Near-optimal; no Belady's |\n| TLB | Hardware cache for page table entries |\n| Virtual memory | Disk extends apparent RAM |\n",
  "dbms-acid-transactions": "# ACID Properties and Transactions\n\n## What is a Transaction?\n\nA **transaction** is a sequence of one or more database operations (reads and writes) that are treated as a single logical unit of work.\n\n**Example:** Bank transfer of \u20b9500 from Account A to Account B:\n1. Read balance of A\n2. Deduct \u20b9500 from A\n3. Read balance of B\n4. Add \u20b9500 to B\n\nAll four steps must succeed together. If step 3 fails after step 2 succeeds, A has lost \u20b9500 with nowhere for it to go. This is why transactions exist \u2014 to ensure atomicity.\n\n---\n\n## ACID Properties\n\nACID stands for: **Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability**\n\nThese four properties guarantee that database transactions are processed reliably even in the face of errors, crashes, and concurrent access.\n\n---\n\n## A \u2014 Atomicity\n\n**\"All or nothing\"**\n\nA transaction either commits completely (all operations succeed) or is completely rolled back (none take effect). There is no partial completion.\n\n**Mechanism:** The DBMS uses a **transaction log (write-ahead log / WAL)**. Before any change is made to the actual database, it is first written to the log. If a crash occurs mid-transaction, the log is used to undo partial changes on recovery.\n\n**Example:** If the bank transfer crashes after debiting A but before crediting B, the DBMS rolls back the debit \u2014 A gets its \u20b9500 back.\n\n---\n\n## C \u2014 Consistency\n\n**\"Database goes from one valid state to another valid state\"**\n\nEvery transaction must leave the database in a state that satisfies all defined rules (constraints, referential integrity, domain rules, etc.).\n\n**Example:** A transaction that violates a foreign key constraint (linking an order to a non-existent customer) is rejected \u2014 the database remains consistent.\n\n**Note:** Consistency is partially the DBMS's job and partially the application's job. The application must write correct business logic.\n\n---\n\n## I \u2014 Isolation\n\n**\"Concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other\"**\n\nEven when many transactions run simultaneously, each transaction behaves as if it is the only one running. Intermediate states of one transaction are invisible to others.\n\n**Why it matters:** Without isolation, one transaction might read data that another transaction is in the middle of modifying \u2014 leading to incorrect results.\n\n---\n\n## D \u2014 Durability\n\n**\"Committed changes survive crashes\"**\n\nOnce a transaction is committed, its changes are permanent \u2014 even if the database server crashes immediately after.\n\n**Mechanism:** Write-ahead logging ensures changes are written to stable storage (disk) before the commit acknowledgment is sent to the user.\n\n---\n\n## Isolation Levels\n\nFull isolation is expensive (requires locking everything). Real systems offer multiple levels as a trade-off between consistency and performance.\n\n### Problems Without Isolation\n\n| Problem | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Dirty Read** | T1 reads data modified by T2 before T2 commits; T2 rolls back \u2192 T1 has wrong data |\n| **Non-Repeatable Read** | T1 reads a row twice; T2 modifies and commits between the two reads \u2192 different values |\n| **Phantom Read** | T1 executes same query twice; T2 inserts rows between them \u2192 different row counts |\n| **Lost Update** | T1 and T2 both read a value, then both update it \u2014 one update is lost |\n\n### Isolation Level Table\n\n| Isolation Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Read Uncommitted** | \u2713 Possible | \u2713 Possible | \u2713 Possible |\n| **Read Committed** | \u2717 Prevented | \u2713 Possible | \u2713 Possible |\n| **Repeatable Read** | \u2717 Prevented | \u2717 Prevented | \u2713 Possible |\n| **Serializable** | \u2717 Prevented | \u2717 Prevented | \u2717 Prevented |\n\n**Serializable** = full isolation = most correct but slowest.\n**Read Committed** = default in most databases (PostgreSQL, Oracle) = good balance.\n\n---\n\n## Transaction States\n\n```\nActive \u2192 Partially Committed \u2192 Committed\n  \u2502                                  \n  \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2192 Failed \u2500\u2500\u2192 Aborted (Rolled Back)\n```\n\n| State | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Active | Transaction is executing |\n| Partially Committed | Last operation completed; awaiting commit |\n| Committed | All changes written to database permanently |\n| Failed | Error occurred; cannot proceed |\n| Aborted | Rolled back; database restored to pre-transaction state |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Property | Guarantee | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| Atomicity | All or nothing | Write-ahead log + rollback |\n| Consistency | Valid state to valid state | Constraints + application logic |\n| Isolation | No interference between concurrent transactions | Locks / MVCC |\n| Durability | Commits survive crashes | Write-ahead log + disk flush |\n",
  "dbms-normalization": "# Normalization\n\n## What is Normalization?\n\nNormalization is the process of organizing a relational database to **reduce data redundancy** and **prevent update anomalies**. It involves decomposing tables into smaller, better-structured tables linked by relationships.\n\n**Why is redundancy bad?**\nSuppose a table stores employee name and department name together. If the department name changes, you need to update every row with that department \u2014 miss one, and the database is inconsistent.\n\n---\n\n## Anomalies (What Normalization Prevents)\n\n| Anomaly | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Insertion Anomaly** | Can't insert partial data | Can't add a department without an employee |\n| **Deletion Anomaly** | Deleting a row removes more info than intended | Deleting last employee deletes department info |\n| **Update Anomaly** | Updating one fact requires changing multiple rows | Rename department in 100 rows, miss one \u2192 inconsistency |\n\n---\n\n## Functional Dependency\n\n**X \u2192 Y** (X determines Y): For every row, if two rows have the same value for X, they must have the same value for Y.\n\n**Example:** EmployeeID \u2192 EmployeeName, Salary, DepartmentID\nKnowing the EmployeeID uniquely determines the employee's name and salary.\n\n---\n\n## First Normal Form (1NF)\n\n**Rule:** Every cell must contain a single, atomic value. No repeating groups.\n\n**Violation example:**\n| StudentID | Name | Subjects |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Alice | Math, Science |\n\n\"Subjects\" is multi-valued \u2192 not 1NF.\n\n**Fixed:**\n| StudentID | Name | Subject |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Alice | Math |\n| 1 | Alice | Science |\n\n**1NF requires:** Atomic values, unique column names, no repeating rows.\n\n---\n\n## Second Normal Form (2NF)\n\n**Rule:** Must be in 1NF. Every non-key attribute must depend on the **entire** primary key (not just part of it).\n\nThis only matters when the primary key is **composite** (multiple columns).\n\n**Violation example:**\nTable: (StudentID, CourseID, CourseName, Marks)\nPrimary key = (StudentID, CourseID)\n\nCourseName depends only on CourseID \u2014 not on (StudentID, CourseID). This is a **partial dependency** \u2192 violates 2NF.\n\n**Fixed:** Split into:\n- StudentCourse(StudentID, CourseID, Marks)\n- Course(CourseID, CourseName)\n\n---\n\n## Third Normal Form (3NF)\n\n**Rule:** Must be in 2NF. No non-key attribute should depend on another non-key attribute (no transitive dependency).\n\n**\"Every non-key attribute must depend on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key.\"**\n\n**Violation example:**\nEmployee(EmpID, DeptID, DeptName)\nPrimary key = EmpID\n\nEmpID \u2192 DeptID (direct). DeptID \u2192 DeptName (DeptName depends on DeptID, not EmpID directly). This is a **transitive dependency**.\n\n**Fixed:** Split into:\n- Employee(EmpID, DeptID)\n- Department(DeptID, DeptName)\n\n---\n\n## Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)\n\n**Rule:** Stricter than 3NF. For every functional dependency X \u2192 Y, X must be a **superkey** (can uniquely identify any row).\n\nMost 3NF tables are also BCNF. The exceptions are rare \u2014 they arise when a table has multiple overlapping candidate keys.\n\n---\n\n## Normal Form Hierarchy\n\n```\n1NF (atomic values)\n  \u2514\u2500\u2500 2NF (no partial dependencies)\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500 3NF (no transitive dependencies)\n              \u2514\u2500\u2500 BCNF (every determinant is a superkey)\n                    \u2514\u2500\u2500 4NF / 5NF (advanced, rarely tested)\n```\n\nHigher normal form = less redundancy, but more tables (more joins needed for queries).\n\n---\n\n## When to Denormalize?\n\nIn OLAP (analytics/reporting) systems, denormalization is intentional \u2014 joining many tables is expensive. Star schema and snowflake schema in data warehouses deliberately keep some redundancy for query speed.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Normal Form | What It Eliminates | Key Concept |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1NF | Multi-valued, non-atomic cells | Atomic values |\n| 2NF | Partial dependency on composite PK | Whole PK dependency |\n| 3NF | Transitive dependency (non-key \u2192 non-key) | Non-key \u2192 key only |\n| BCNF | Every FD's LHS must be superkey | Stronger than 3NF |\n",
  "dbms-indexing": "# Indexing and Query Optimization\n\n## What is an Index?\n\nAn **index** is a separate data structure that stores a subset of a table's columns in a way that speeds up data retrieval. Think of it like the index at the back of a textbook \u2014 instead of reading every page to find \"recursion\", you look it up in the index and jump directly to the right pages.\n\n**Trade-off:** Indexes speed up reads but slow down writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE must also update the index).\n\n---\n\n## Why Indexes Are Needed\n\nWithout an index, every query against a table is a **full table scan** \u2014 the DBMS reads every row to find matching ones. For a table with 1 million rows, this is extremely slow.\n\nWith an index on the searched column, the DBMS can jump directly to matching rows in O(log n) time.\n\n---\n\n## Types of Indexes\n\n### Clustered Index\n- The physical order of rows in the table matches the index order\n- **Only one clustered index per table** (because there's only one physical order)\n- Typically created on the primary key automatically\n- Range queries (WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 30) are very efficient\n\n### Non-Clustered Index\n- A separate structure with pointers back to the actual rows\n- **Multiple non-clustered indexes allowed** per table\n- Lookup requires following the pointer to the actual row (extra step)\n- Best for columns frequently used in WHERE, JOIN ON, ORDER BY\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Feature | Clustered | Non-Clustered |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data order | Matches index | Separate structure |\n| Count per table | Max 1 | Many |\n| Range queries | Very fast | Moderate |\n| Extra storage | No | Yes |\n\n---\n\n## Dense vs Sparse Index\n\n| Type | Entry For | Size | Use Case |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dense** | Every record in the file | Large | Fast lookup |\n| **Sparse** | Every block/page | Small | Ordered files only |\n\n---\n\n## B-Tree Index\n\nA **B-Tree** (Balanced Tree) is the most common index structure. All paths from root to leaf have the same length (balanced).\n\n**Properties:**\n- Each node has multiple keys and multiple children\n- Keys are sorted in each node\n- All values stored at all levels\n- Good for point queries (find one row)\n\n**B+ Tree** (most databases use this, not plain B-Tree):\n- All data pointers are ONLY in leaf nodes\n- Internal nodes contain only keys (more keys per node \u2192 shorter tree)\n- Leaf nodes are linked in a chain \u2192 range scans are efficient\n- Used by MySQL InnoDB, PostgreSQL, Oracle\n\n---\n\n## Hash Index\n\nBased on a hash function. Maps column value \u2192 bucket.\n\n**Pros:** O(1) exact match lookup  \n**Cons:** Cannot do range queries, sensitive to hash collisions  \n**Use when:** Only equality comparisons (=) needed, not range or ORDER BY\n\n---\n\n## When to Create an Index\n\n**Create an index on:**\n- Primary and foreign key columns (usually done automatically)\n- Columns frequently used in WHERE clauses\n- Columns used in JOIN conditions\n- Columns used in ORDER BY or GROUP BY\n\n**Avoid creating an index on:**\n- Small tables (full scan is fast enough; index overhead not worth it)\n- Columns with very low cardinality (e.g., gender has only 2 values \u2014 half the table matches; index doesn't help)\n- Columns that are updated very frequently\n\n---\n\n## Query Optimization Basics\n\nThe **query optimizer** is the DBMS component that determines the most efficient way to execute a SQL query.\n\n**EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN PLAN:** Shows how the optimizer plans to execute a query. Indicates whether indexes are used or a full table scan is happening.\n\n**Common optimizations:**\n- Write ",
  "cn-osi-tcpip": "# OSI and TCP/IP Models\n\n## Why Do We Need a Network Model?\n\nWhen two computers communicate, they need to agree on hundreds of details: how bits are transmitted, how errors are detected, how data is addressed, how applications identify each other. A **network model** divides this complexity into layers, where each layer handles one specific concern and communicates only with the layers above and below it.\n\n---\n\n## The OSI Model \u2014 7 Layers\n\nOSI (Open Systems Interconnection) is the theoretical reference model. Real networks use TCP/IP, but OSI is used for understanding and troubleshooting.\n\n| Layer | Number | Name | Key Protocols/Devices | Data Unit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Application | 7 | Application | HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SSH | Data |\n| Presentation | 6 | Presentation | SSL/TLS, JPEG, MPEG | Data |\n| Session | 5 | Session | NetBIOS, RPC | Data |\n| Transport | 4 | Transport | TCP, UDP | Segment |\n| Network | 3 | Network | IP, ICMP, RIP, OSPF | Packet |\n| Data Link | 2 | Data Link | Ethernet, Wi-Fi, MAC | Frame |\n| Physical | 1 | Physical | Cables, Hubs, Repeaters | Bits |\n\n**Mnemonic (top to bottom):** **A**ll **P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing\n\n---\n\n## Layer by Layer Explanation\n\n### Layer 7 \u2014 Application Layer\nThe layer you interact with directly. Defines protocols for specific applications.\n- HTTP/HTTPS: Web browsing\n- SMTP/POP3/IMAP: Email\n- FTP/SFTP: File transfer\n- DNS: Domain name to IP resolution\n- SSH: Secure remote access\n\n### Layer 6 \u2014 Presentation Layer\nHandles data format translation between application and network.\n- **Encryption/Decryption:** SSL/TLS encrypts data here\n- **Compression:** Reduces data size\n- **Translation:** Character encoding (ASCII, UTF-8)\n\n### Layer 5 \u2014 Session Layer\nManages sessions (long-running dialogues) between applications.\n- Establishes, maintains, and terminates sessions\n- Handles authentication and reconnection\n- Example: NetBIOS for Windows file sharing\n\n### Layer 4 \u2014 Transport Layer\nEnd-to-end communication between processes. Uses **port numbers** to identify which application on a host.\n- **TCP:** Reliable, ordered, connection-oriented\n- **UDP:** Unreliable, fast, connectionless\n- **Ports:** 0\u20131023 (well-known), 1024\u201349151 (registered), 49152\u201365535 (dynamic)\n\n### Layer 3 \u2014 Network Layer\nLogical addressing and routing between different networks. Decides the best path.\n- **IP (Internet Protocol):** Addressing (IPv4, IPv6)\n- **ICMP:** Error messages and diagnostics (ping uses this)\n- **Routers** operate at this layer\n- **Routing protocols:** RIP, OSPF, BGP\n\n### Layer 2 \u2014 Data Link Layer\nNode-to-node communication on the same network segment. Uses **MAC addresses**.\n- **Ethernet:** Wired LAN standard\n- **Wi-Fi (802.11):** Wireless LAN\n- **Switches** and **bridges** operate at this layer\n- Adds frame header and trailer (includes error detection via CRC)\n\n### Layer 1 \u2014 Physical Layer\nActual transmission of raw bits over a physical medium.\n- Cables (Cat5, fiber optic), radio waves (Wi-Fi)\n- **Hubs** operate at this layer\n- Deals with voltage levels, timing, pin layouts\n\n---\n\n## Encapsulation \u2014 How Data Travels\n\nEach layer adds its own **header** (and sometimes trailer) as data travels down the stack, and strips them as data travels up on the receiving side.\n\n```\nApplication:  [DATA]\nTransport:    [TCP Header] [DATA]\nNetwork:      [IP Header] [TCP Header] [DATA]\nData Link:    [Frame Hdr] [IP Header] [TCP Header] [DATA] [Frame Trailer]\nPhysical:     0101010101010101010... (bits)\n```\n\n---\n\n## The TCP/IP Model \u2014 4 Layers\n\nThe practical model used by the real Internet. Maps to OSI as follows:\n\n| TCP/IP Layer | OSI Equivalent | Protocols |\n|---|---|---|\n| Application | Layers 5, 6, 7 | HTTP, DNS, FTP, SMTP |\n| Transport | Layer 4 | TCP, UDP |\n| Internet | Layer 3 | IP, ICMP |\n| Network Access | Layers 1, 2 | Ethernet, Wi-Fi |\n\n---\n\n## Key Protocols \u2014 Which Layer?\n\n| Protocol | Layer | Function |\n|---|---|---|\n| HTTP/HTTPS | Application | Web |\n| FTP | Application | File transfer |\n| DNS | Application | Name resolution |\n| TCP | Transport | Reliable delivery |\n| UDP | Transport | Fast delivery |\n| IP | Network | Addressing & routing |\n| ICMP | Network | Error messages (ping) |\n| ARP | Data Link / Network border | IP \u2192 MAC resolution |\n| Ethernet | Data Link | LAN frames |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Layer | Mnemonic Word | Devices | Address Type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 Application | All | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| 6 Presentation | People | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| 5 Session | Seem | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| 4 Transport | To | \u2014 | Port number |\n| 3 Network | Need | Router | IP address |\n| 2 Data Link | Data | Switch, Bridge | MAC address |\n| 1 Physical | Processing | Hub, Repeater | \u2014 |\n",
  "cn-tcp-udp": "# TCP vs UDP\n\n## Overview\n\nTCP and UDP are the two main transport layer protocols. They both use port numbers to direct traffic to the right application, but they have fundamentally different approaches to delivering data.\n\n**Simple summary:**\n- **TCP** = Reliable, ordered, connection-oriented. Slower but guaranteed delivery.\n- **UDP** = Fast, connectionless, no guarantees. Used when speed matters more than reliability.\n\n---\n\n## TCP \u2014 Transmission Control Protocol\n\n### What TCP Guarantees\n- **Reliable delivery:** Every segment is acknowledged. Lost segments are retransmitted.\n- **Ordered delivery:** Sequence numbers ensure segments are reassembled in the correct order even if they arrive out of order.\n- **Error checking:** Each segment has a checksum.\n- **Flow control:** Receiver tells sender how much data it can handle (sliding window).\n- **Congestion control:** TCP slows down when the network is congested (slow start, AIMD).\n\n### TCP Three-Way Handshake (Connection Setup)\n\nBefore any data is sent, TCP establishes a connection:\n\n```\nClient                          Server\n  \u2502                               \u2502\n  \u2502\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 SYN (seq=x) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba\u2502  Step 1: Client says \"I want to connect\"\n  \u2502                               \u2502\n  \u2502\u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 SYN-ACK (seq=y, ack=x+1)\u2500\u2502  Step 2: Server accepts and sends its seq\n  \u2502                               \u2502\n  \u2502\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 ACK (ack=y+1) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba\u2502  Step 3: Client confirms; connection open\n  \u2502                               \u2502\n  \u2502\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 DATA TRANSFER \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2502\n```\n\n- **SYN** (Synchronize): Initiate connection, share initial sequence number\n- **SYN-ACK**: Accept connection, share server's sequence number\n- **ACK**: Acknowledge server's SYN\n\n### TCP Four-Way Termination (Connection Close)\n\n```\nClient                          Server\n  \u2502\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 FIN \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba\u2502  \"I'm done sending\"\n  \u2502\u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 ACK \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 \u2502  \"Got it\"\n  \u2502\u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 FIN \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 \u2502  \"I'm done too\"\n  \u2502\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 ACK \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba\u2502  \"Confirmed\"\n  \u2502 (enters TIME_WAIT state)     \u2502\n```\n\n**TIME_WAIT:** Client waits 2\u00d7MSL (Maximum Segment Lifetime, ~60 seconds) before fully closing. This ensures any delayed packets from the old connection don't confuse a new connection on the same port.\n\n### TCP Applications\n\nHTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, SSH, database connections \u2014 anywhere data integrity is critical.\n\n---\n\n## UDP \u2014 User Datagram Protocol\n\n### What UDP Does NOT Provide\n- No connection setup (connectionless)\n- No acknowledgments\n- No retransmission\n- No ordering guarantee\n- No flow control or congestion control\n\n### What UDP IS Good For\n- **Low latency:** No connection setup delay, no waiting for ACKs\n- **Broadcast/Multicast:** Sending to multiple receivers simultaneously\n- **Simple request-response:** Single packet sent, single packet back (DNS queries)\n- **Tolerates loss:** A lost video frame is better than a delayed one\n\n### UDP Applications\n\n| Application | Why UDP |\n|---|---|\n| DNS | Small queries; retried by application if no response |\n| VoIP/Video calls | Dropped packets are tolerable; delays are not |\n| Live video streaming | Old frames are useless; just keep playing |\n| Online gaming | Position updates are time-sensitive |\n| DHCP | Broadcast to find the server |\n\n---\n\n## Comparison Table\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Connection | Connection-oriented (handshake) | Connectionless |\n| Reliability | Guaranteed delivery | Best-effort (no guarantee) |\n| Ordering | Ordered | Unordered |\n| Error checking | Checksum + retransmission | Checksum only |\n| Flow control | Yes (sliding window) | No |\n| Congestion control | Yes | No |\n| Speed | Slower | Faster |\n| Overhead | Large header (20 bytes minimum) | Small header (8 bytes) |\n| Use case | File transfer, web, email | Gaming, streaming, DNS |\n\n---\n\n## Well-Known Port Numbers\n\n| Port | Protocol | Layer | Service |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 20, 21 | FTP | TCP | File transfer |\n| 22 | SSH | TCP | Secure shell |\n| 23 | Telnet | TCP | Remote login (unsecured) |\n| 25 | SMTP | TCP | Email sending |\n| 53 | DNS | UDP (primarily) | Domain name resolution |\n| 67, 68 | DHCP | UDP | IP address assignment |\n| 80 | HTTP | TCP | Web |\n| 110 | POP3 | TCP | Email retrieval |\n| 143 | IMAP | TCP | Email retrieval (modern) |\n| 443 | HTTPS | TCP | Secure web |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Aspect | TCP | UDP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Acronym | Transmission Control Protocol | User Datagram Protocol |\n| Connection | 3-way handshake | None |\n| Reliable? | Yes (ACK + retransmit) | No |\n| Ordered? | Yes (sequence numbers) | No |\n| Speed | Slower | Faster |\n| Best for | HTTP, FTP, SSH, email | DNS, VoIP, gaming, streaming |\n",
  "cn-ip-subnetting": "# IP Addressing and Subnetting\n\n## What is an IP Address?\n\nAn **IP address** (Internet Protocol address) is a numerical label assigned to every device on a network. It serves two purposes:\n1. **Host identification** \u2014 which specific device?\n2. **Location addressing** \u2014 where on the network?\n\nEvery device that communicates on the internet needs a unique IP address.\n\n---\n\n## IPv4 Basics\n\nIPv4 addresses are **32 bits** long, written as four groups of 8 bits (octets) in decimal:\n**192.168.1.1** = 11000000.10101000.00000001.00000001\n\nRange: 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255 (~4.3 billion addresses)\n\n---\n\n## IP Address Classes\n\nHistorically, the address space was divided into classes. This is now replaced by CIDR, but classes still appear in exams.\n\n| Class | First Octet Range | Default Subnet Mask | Network / Host Bits | Usage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 1 \u2013 126 | 255.0.0.0 (/8) | 8 network / 24 host | Very large networks |\n| B | 128 \u2013 191 | 255.255.0.0 (/16) | 16 network / 16 host | Medium networks |\n| C | 192 \u2013 223 | 255.255.255.0 (/24) | 24 network / 8 host | Small networks |\n| D | 224 \u2013 239 | \u2014 | Multicast | Group communication |\n| E | 240 \u2013 255 | \u2014 | Experimental | Reserved |\n\n**Special address:** 127.0.0.1 = loopback (localhost) \u2014 never leaves the machine.\n\n---\n\n## Private IP Addresses (RFC 1918)\n\nThese ranges are not routable on the public internet. Used in home/office networks behind a NAT router:\n\n| Range | CIDR | Class |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10.0.0.0 \u2013 10.255.255.255 | /8 | A |\n| 172.16.0.0 \u2013 172.31.255.255 | /12 | B |\n| 192.168.0.0 \u2013 192.168.255.255 | /16 | C |\n\nYour home router likely gives you 192.168.x.x addresses.\n\n---\n\n## Subnet Mask and CIDR\n\nA **subnet mask** tells you which part of an IP address is the network portion and which is the host portion.\n\n**Example:** 255.255.255.0 = 11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000\n- First 24 bits = network\n- Last 8 bits = host\n- Written as **/24** in CIDR notation\n\n**CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing):** Write address/prefix as IP/n where n = number of network bits.\n\n---\n\n## Subnetting \u2014 The Key Formulas\n\nGiven a network with prefix /n:\n- **Host bits** = 32 \u2212 n\n- **Total addresses** = 2^(32\u2212n)\n- **Usable host addresses** = 2^(32\u2212n) \u2212 2\n\nWhy subtract 2? One address is the **network address** (all host bits = 0) and one is the **broadcast address** (all host bits = 1).\n\n| CIDR | Host Bits | Total | Usable Hosts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| /24 | 8 | 256 | 254 |\n| /25 | 7 | 128 | 126 |\n| /26 | 6 | 64 | 62 |\n| /27 | 5 | 32 | 30 |\n| /28 | 4 | 16 | 14 |\n| /29 | 3 | 8 | 6 |\n| /30 | 2 | 4 | 2 |\n\n---\n\n## Subnetting Example\n\n**Problem:** Divide 192.168.1.0/24 into 4 equal subnets.\n\n**Step 1:** 4 subnets require 2 extra bits (2\u00b2 = 4). New prefix = /24 + 2 = /26.\n\n**Step 2:** Each subnet has 2\u2076 = 64 addresses, 62 usable.\n\n**Step 3:** The 4 subnets:\n| Subnet | Network Address | Broadcast | Usable Range |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 192.168.1.0 | 192.168.1.63 | .1 \u2013 .62 |\n| 2 | 192.168.1.64 | 192.168.1.127 | .65 \u2013 .126 |\n| 3 | 192.168.1.128 | 192.168.1.191 | .129 \u2013 .190 |\n| 4 | 192.168.1.192 | 192.168.1.255 | .193 \u2013 .254 |\n\n---\n\n## IPv6 \u2014 Brief Overview\n\nIPv6 was created to solve IPv4 exhaustion. It uses **128-bit addresses** written in hexadecimal:\n**2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334**\n\n- 2\u00b9\u00b2\u2078 \u2248 340 undecillion addresses\n- No need for NAT\n- Built-in IPSec security\n- Simplified header format\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Concept | Key Point |\n|---|---|\n| IPv4 | 32-bit address, 4.3 billion total |\n| Private ranges | 10.x, 172.16\u201331.x, 192.168.x \u2014 not internet-routable |\n| 127.0.0.1 | Loopback (localhost) |\n| /n notation | n bits for network, 32\u2212n for host |\n| Usable hosts | 2^(32\u2212n) \u2212 2 |\n| Subnet 0 | Network address |\n| Last subnet addr | Broadcast address |\n",
  "oops-principles": "# OOP Principles \u2014 Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism\n\n## What is OOP?\n\nObject-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that organizes code around **objects** \u2014 self-contained units that bundle data (attributes) and behaviors (methods) together.\n\nInstead of thinking about procedures that operate on data, you think about objects that know things (data) and can do things (methods).\n\nThe four core principles are: **Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Encapsulation \u2014 \"Protect Your Data\"\n\n**Definition:** Bundling the data (fields) and the methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), and restricting direct access to the internal state.\n\n**Why it matters:** Without encapsulation, any part of the program could change an object's data directly, leading to unpredictable behavior.\n\n**Access Modifiers (Java):**\n\n| Modifier | Same Class | Same Package | Subclass | Everywhere |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| private | \u2713 | \u2717 | \u2717 | \u2717 |\n| default | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2717 | \u2717 |\n| protected | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2717 |\n| public | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2713 |\n\n**Real example:**\n```java\nclass BankAccount {\n    private double balance;  // can't be modified directly\n\n    public void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount > 0) balance += amount;  // controlled access\n    }\n\n    public double getBalance() {\n        return balance;  // read-only access\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe balance field is private. Outsiders can't set it to a negative value directly. They must use the deposit/withdraw methods which enforce business rules.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Abstraction \u2014 \"Show What's Needed, Hide How It Works\"\n\n**Definition:** Exposing only essential features of an object while hiding implementation complexity.\n\n**Analogy:** You use a TV remote without knowing the electronics inside. The remote's interface (buttons) is the abstraction; the circuitry is the hidden implementation.\n\n**In Java \u2014 Two ways to achieve abstraction:**\n\n**Abstract Class:** A class that cannot be instantiated, may have abstract methods (no body) that subclasses must implement.\n\n```java\nabstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();    // no implementation\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Area: \" + area());  // uses the abstract method\n    }\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    double radius;\n    double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }  // must implement\n}\n```\n\n**Interface:** A contract \u2014 all methods are abstract by default. A class can implement multiple interfaces.\n\n```java\ninterface Drawable { void draw(); }\ninterface Resizable { void resize(int factor); }\nclass Square implements Drawable, Resizable { ... }\n```\n\n**Difference \u2014 Abstract Class vs Interface:**\n\n| Feature | Abstract Class | Interface |\n|---|---|---|\n| Instantiation | Cannot | Cannot |\n| Method types | Abstract + concrete | Abstract (Java 8+: default, static) |\n| Multiple inheritance | Not supported | Supported (implement many) |\n| Has state | Yes (instance variables) | No (only constants) |\n| Use when | Common base + shared code | Defining capability/contract |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Inheritance \u2014 \"Reuse and Extend\"\n\n**Definition:** A class (child/subclass) acquires properties and behaviors from another class (parent/superclass).\n\n**Keyword:** `extends` in Java; `:` in C++\n\n**Represents:** \"IS-A\" relationship.\n- Dog IS-A Animal\n- Car IS-A Vehicle\n- CheckingAccount IS-A BankAccount\n\n```java\nclass Animal {\n    String name;\n    void eat() { System.out.println(name + \" is eating\"); }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() { System.out.println(name + \" is barking\"); }\n}\n\nDog d = new Dog();\nd.name = \"Rex\";\nd.eat();   // inherited from Animal\nd.bark();  // Dog's own method\n```\n\n**Types of Inheritance:**\n\n| Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Single | One class inherits from one parent |\n| Multilevel | A inherits B, B inherits C (chain) |\n| Hierarchical | Multiple classes inherit from same parent |\n| Multiple | One class inherits from multiple parents (C++ only; Java uses interfaces) |\n| Hybrid | Combination of above |\n\n**Why Java doesn't support multiple class inheritance:** The **Diamond Problem** \u2014 if B and C both define method foo(), and D inherits both, which foo() does D get? Ambiguous. Java avoids this by only allowing single class inheritance (but multiple interface implementation).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Polymorphism \u2014 \"Many Forms\"\n\n**Definition:** The ability of a single interface or method name to represent different behaviors depending on context.\n\n### Compile-time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)\nSame method name, different parameter lists. Resolved at compile time.\n\n```java\nvoid print(int x) { ... }\nvoid print(String s) { ... }\nvoid print(double d) { ... }\n// Java picks the right one based on argument types at compile time\n```\n\n### Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)\nChild class provides its own implementation of a parent class method. Resolved at runtime based on the actual object type.\n\n```java\nAnimal a = new Dog();  // reference type = Animal, object type = Dog\na.speak();             // calls Dog's speak(), not Animal's!\n```\n\nThe decision of which `speak()` to call happens at runtime \u2014 Java looks at the actual object (Dog), not the reference type (Animal). This is called **dynamic dispatch**.\n\n**Overloading vs Overriding:**\n\n| Feature | Overloading | Overriding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where | Same class | Parent + Child class |\n| Signature | Different parameters | Same signature |\n| Binding | Compile-time | Runtime |\n| Access modifier | Can change | Cannot be more restrictive |\n| Return type | Can differ | Must be same (or covariant) |\n| static methods | Can overload | Cannot override (only hide) |\n\n---\n\n## SOLID Principles (Brief)\n\nBuilt on top of OOP principles, these 5 rules guide good object-oriented design:\n\n| Letter | Principle | Summary |\n|---|---|---|\n| S | Single Responsibility | A class should have only one reason to change |\n| O | Open/Closed | Open for extension, closed for modification |\n| L | Liskov Substitution | Subclasses must be usable wherever parent is used |\n| I | Interface Segregation | Many specific interfaces > one general interface |\n| D | Dependency Inversion | Depend on abstractions, not concrete classes |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Principle | Keyword | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| Encapsulation | Data hiding | private/protected + getters/setters |\n| Abstraction | Hide complexity | abstract class + interface |\n| Inheritance | Code reuse | extends / IS-A |\n| Polymorphism (compile) | Overloading | Same name, different params |\n| Polymorphism (runtime) | Overriding | Child redefines parent method |\n",
  "oops-classes-objects": "# Classes, Objects, and Constructors\n\n## What is a Class?\n\nA **class** is a blueprint or template. It defines what attributes (data) and methods (behaviors) objects of that type will have. A class itself is not a real thing \u2014 it's the design.\n\n**Analogy:** A class is like an architectural blueprint. The blueprint for \"House\" defines it has walls, rooms, and a roof. Each actual house built from it is an object.\n\n---\n\n## What is an Object?\n\nAn **object** is an instance of a class \u2014 a concrete, real thing created in memory based on the class template.\n\n```java\n// Class definition (blueprint)\nclass Car {\n    String brand;\n    int speed;\n    void accelerate() { speed += 10; }\n}\n\n// Creating objects (instances)\nCar car1 = new Car();  // Object 1 created in heap memory\nCar car2 = new Car();  // Object 2 \u2014 completely separate\ncar1.brand = \"Toyota\";\ncar2.brand = \"Honda\";\n```\n\nEvery object gets its own copy of instance variables. car1.brand and car2.brand are independent.\n\n---\n\n## Memory Layout\n\nWhen a Java/C++ program runs:\n- **Stack:** Stores local variables, method calls, and references to objects\n- **Heap:** Stores the actual objects (instance variables live here)\n\n```\nStack              Heap\ncar1 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba  [brand=\"Toyota\", speed=0]\ncar2 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba  [brand=\"Honda\", speed=0]\n```\n\nThe variable car1 on the stack holds a **reference** (memory address) to the object on the heap.\n\n---\n\n## Constructors\n\nA **constructor** is a special method called automatically when an object is created with `new`. It initializes the object's state.\n\n**Rules:**\n- Same name as the class\n- No return type (not even void)\n- Called once \u2014 at object creation\n- If you don't write one, Java provides a default (no-arg, does nothing)\n\n### Types of Constructors\n\n**Default Constructor:** No parameters. Java provides one automatically if you define none.\n```java\nclass Student {\n    String name;\n    // Java auto-provides: Student() { }\n}\n```\n\n**Parameterized Constructor:** Takes arguments to set initial values.\n```java\nclass Student {\n    String name;\n    int age;\n    Student(String name, int age) {\n        this.name = name;  // 'this' refers to current object\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n}\nStudent s = new Student(\"Arjun\", 20);\n```\n\n**Copy Constructor (C++):** Creates a new object as a copy of an existing one.\n```cpp\nStudent(const Student& other) {\n    this->name = other.name;\n    this->age = other.age;\n}\nStudent s2 = s1;  // calls copy constructor\n```\n\n### Constructor Overloading\nYou can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists:\n```java\nStudent() { this.name = \"Unknown\"; this.age = 0; }\nStudent(String name) { this.name = name; this.age = 0; }\nStudent(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }\n```\n\n---\n\n## The `this` Keyword\n\n`this` refers to the current object \u2014 the object on which the method or constructor was called.\n\n**Use case 1 \u2014 Disambiguate field vs parameter:**\n```java\nStudent(String name) {\n    this.name = name;  // this.name = field; name = parameter\n}\n```\n\n**Use case 2 \u2014 Call another constructor:**\n```java\nStudent(String name) {\n    this(name, 0);  // calls Student(String, int) constructor\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Static Members\n\n**Static variable:** Belongs to the class, not any instance. Shared across all objects.\n**Static method:** Can be called without creating an object. Cannot access non-static (instance) members.\n\n```java\nclass Counter {\n    static int count = 0;  // shared by all Counter objects\n    Counter() { count++; }\n    static int getCount() { return count; }\n}\nCounter c1 = new Counter();\nCounter c2 = new Counter();\nSystem.out.println(Counter.getCount());  // 2\n```\n\n**Why can't static methods access instance variables?**\nStatic methods exist at the class level. When you call Counter.getCount(), there might be zero Counter objects. There's no \"this\" to refer to \u2014 no specific object's data to access.\n\n---\n\n## Destructor / Garbage Collection\n\n**C++:** Destructor (~ClassName) is called when an object goes out of scope or is explicitly deleted. You clean up resources (close files, free memory) here.\n\n**Java:** No explicit destructor. The **Garbage Collector (GC)** automatically frees objects that are no longer referenced. finalize() was a near-destructor but is deprecated \u2014 use try-with-resources or close() instead.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Concept | Key Point |\n|---|---|\n| Class | Blueprint (template) \u2014 no memory allocated |\n| Object | Instance of class \u2014 memory allocated on heap |\n| Constructor | Initializes object at creation; same name as class, no return type |\n| `this` | Refers to the current object |\n| Static variable | Shared across all instances |\n| Static method | No `this`; can't access instance variables |\n| Garbage collection | Java auto-frees unreachable objects |\n",
  "oops-exceptions-patterns": "# Exception Handling and Design Patterns\n\n## Part 1: Exception Handling\n\n### What is an Exception?\n\nAn **exception** is an unexpected event that disrupts the normal flow of a program. Examples: file not found, division by zero, null pointer access, network timeout.\n\nWithout exception handling, programs crash when errors occur. With it, programs can detect, report, and recover from errors gracefully.\n\n---\n\n### Exception Hierarchy (Java)\n\n```\nThrowable\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Error (serious system errors \u2014 don't catch these)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 OutOfMemoryError\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 StackOverflowError\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 Exception\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 RuntimeException (Unchecked)\n    \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 NullPointerException\n    \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException\n    \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 ClassCastException\n    \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ArithmeticException\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 IOException (Checked)\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500 FileNotFoundException\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500 SQLException\n```\n\n### Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions\n\n| Feature | Checked | Unchecked |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base class | Exception | RuntimeException |\n| Must handle? | Yes \u2014 declare with throws or catch | No |\n| When they occur | External failures (file, network, DB) | Programming bugs |\n| Examples | IOException, SQLException | NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds |\n\n---\n\n### try-catch-finally\n\n```java\ntry {\n    // Code that might throw an exception\n    int result = 10 / 0;\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n    // Handle the specific exception\n    System.out.println(\"Cannot divide by zero: \" + e.getMessage());\n} catch (Exception e) {\n    // Catch any other exception (more general \u2014 put LAST)\n    System.out.println(\"Something went wrong\");\n} finally {\n    // ALWAYS executes \u2014 for cleanup (close files, connections)\n    System.out.println(\"This runs no matter what\");\n}\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Catch more specific exceptions BEFORE more general ones (or the specific catch is unreachable)\n- finally always runs, even if a return statement is in try or catch\n- try-with-resources (Java 7+): `try (FileReader fr = new FileReader(\"file.txt\"))` \u2014 auto-closes resource\n\n### throw vs throws\n\n| Keyword | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `throw` | Create and raise an exception: `throw new IOException(\"File not found\");` |\n| `throws` | Declare that a method might throw: `void readFile() throws IOException {}` |\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Design Patterns\n\nDesign patterns are reusable solutions to commonly occurring problems in software design. They're not code \u2014 they're templates you adapt to your situation.\n\nThree categories: **Creational, Structural, Behavioral**\n\n---\n\n### Singleton Pattern (Creational)\n\n**Problem:** Ensure a class has exactly ONE instance, and provide a global access point to it.\n\n**Use cases:** Logger, configuration manager, thread pool, database connection pool.\n\n**Implementation:**\n```java\nclass Logger {\n    private static Logger instance;  // the single instance\n    private Logger() {}              // private constructor \u2014 prevents new Logger()\n\n    public static Logger getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {\n            instance = new Logger();\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n\n    public void log(String msg) { System.out.println(msg); }\n}\n\n// Usage\nLogger.getInstance().log(\"Server started\");\n```\n\n**Thread-safe version (double-checked locking):**\n```java\npublic static Logger getInstance() {\n    if (instance == null) {\n        synchronized (Logger.class) {\n            if (instance == null) {\n                instance = new Logger();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return instance;\n}\n```\n\n**Simplest thread-safe version (enum):**\n```java\nenum Logger { INSTANCE; public void log(String msg) {...} }\nLogger.INSTANCE.log(\"Started\");\n```\n\n---\n\n### Factory Pattern (Creational)\n\n**Problem:** Create objects without specifying the exact class. The factory decides which subclass to instantiate.\n\n```java\ninterface Shape { void draw(); }\nclass Circle implements Shape { public void draw() { System.out.println(\"Drawing Circle\"); } }\nclass Square implements Shape { public void draw() { System.out.println(\"Drawing Square\"); } }\n\nclass ShapeFactory {\n    public static Shape create(String type) {\n        if (type.equals(\"circle\")) return new Circle();\n        if (type.equals(\"square\")) return new Square();\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown shape\");\n    }\n}\n\nShape s = ShapeFactory.create(\"circle\");  // client doesn't know it's a Circle\ns.draw();\n```\n\n---\n\n### Observer Pattern (Behavioral)\n\n**Problem:** When one object changes state, all dependent objects are automatically notified.\n\n**Use cases:** Event listeners (button clicks), MVC (View observes Model), publish-subscribe systems.\n\n```java\ninterface Observer { void update(String event); }\n\nclass EventSource {\n    List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();\n    void subscribe(Observer o) { observers.add(o); }\n    void notifyAll(String event) {\n        for (Observer o : observers) o.update(event);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Strategy Pattern (Behavioral)\n\n**Problem:** Define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each, make them interchangeable.\n\n```java\ninterface SortStrategy { void sort(int[] arr); }\nclass BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { public void sort(int[] arr) {...} }\nclass QuickSort  implements SortStrategy { public void sort(int[] arr) {...} }\n\nclass Sorter {\n    SortStrategy strategy;\n    Sorter(SortStrategy s) { this.strategy = s; }\n    void sort(int[] arr) { strategy.sort(arr); }\n}\n\nSorter s = new Sorter(new QuickSort());\ns.sort(data);\n```\n\n---\n\n### Pattern Summary\n\n| Pattern | Category | Problem Solved |\n|---|---|---|\n| Singleton | Creational | One instance; global access |\n| Factory | Creational | Create objects without specifying class |\n| Abstract Factory | Creational | Create families of related objects |\n| Observer | Behavioral | Notify dependents of state changes |\n| Strategy | Behavioral | Swap algorithms at runtime |\n| Decorator | Structural | Add behavior without inheritance |\n| Adapter | Structural | Make incompatible interfaces work together |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Concept | Key Point |\n|---|---|\n| Checked exception | Must declare or catch; extends Exception |\n| Unchecked exception | Programming bug; extends RuntimeException |\n| finally | Always runs; use for cleanup |\n| throw | Raises an exception |\n| throws | Declares that a method may throw |\n| Singleton | Private constructor + static instance + getInstance() |\n| Factory | Centralized object creation; client doesn't know concrete class |\n| Observer | Publisher notifies subscribers on state change |\n"
};